    "subtopic": "Differentiation of One Function with respect to Another",
    "difficulty": "MODERATE",
    "stem": "Find the derivative of \\(\\cos^{-1} x\\) w.r.t. \\(\\sqrt{1-x^2}\\).",
    "solution": "Let \\(u = \\cos^{-1} x\\) and \\(v = \\sqrt{1-x^2}\\), then we have to find \\(\\frac{du}{dv}\\).\ni.e. \\(\\frac{du}{dv} = \\frac{\\frac{du}{dx}}{\\frac{dv}{dx}}\\) ... (I)\nNow, \\(u = \\cos^{-1} x\\)\nDifferentiate w.r.t. \\(x\\)\n\\(\\frac{du}{dx} = \\frac{d}{dx}(\\cos^{-1} x) = -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}\\) ... (II)\nAnd, \\(v = \\sqrt{1-x^2}\\)\nDifferentiate w.r.t. \\(x\\)\n\\(\\frac{dv}{dx} = \\frac{d}{dx}\\left(\\sqrt{1-x^2}\\right) = \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{1-x^2}} \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(1-x^2) = \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{1-x^2}} \\cdot (-2x)\\)\n\\(\\frac{dv}{dx} = -\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}\\) ... (III)\nSubstituting (II) and (III) in (I) we get,\n\\(\\frac{du}{dv} = \\frac{-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}}{-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}}\\)\n\\(\\therefore \\frac{du}{dv} = \\frac{1}{x}\\)"
  },
  {
    "ref": "1.4.3 SolvedEx.3",
    "bucket": "solved",
    "format": "subjective",
    "subtopic": "Differentiation of One Function with respect to Another",
    "difficulty": "HARD",
    "stem": "Find the derivative of \\(\\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{1+x^2}-1}{x}\\right)\\) w.r.t. \\(\\sin^{-1}\\left(\\frac{2x}{1+x^2}\\right)\\).",
    "solution": "[Textbook misprint: the book's printed solution drops the \\(\\tan^{-1}\\) operator from the middle expression of its simplification chain. The surrounding derivation and the printed final answer \\(\\frac{du}{dv}=\\frac{1}{4}\\) are correct.]\n\nLet \\(u = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{1+x^2}-1}{x}\\right)\\) and \\(v = \\sin^{-1}\\left(\\frac{2x}{1+x^2}\\right)\\), then we have to find \\(\\frac{du}{dv}\\).\ni.e. \\(\\frac{du}{dv} = \\frac{\\frac{du}{dx}}{\\frac{dv}{dx}}\\) ... (I)\nNow, \\(u = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{1+x^2}-1}{x}\\right)\\)\nPut \\(x = \\tan \\theta\\) \\(\\therefore \\theta = \\tan^{-1} x\\)\n\\(u = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{1+\\tan^2 \\theta}-1}{\\tan \\theta}\\right) = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{\\sec \\theta - 1}{\\tan \\theta}\\right) = \\left(\\frac{\\frac{1}{\\cos \\theta}-1}{\\frac{\\sin \\theta}{\\cos \\theta}}\\right) = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{1-\\cos \\theta}{\\sin \\theta}\\right)\\)\n\\(= \\tan^{-1}\\left[\\frac{2 \\sin^2\\left(\\frac{\\theta}{2}\\right)}{2 \\sin\\left(\\frac{\\theta}{2}\\right) \\cos\\left(\\frac{\\theta}{2}\\right)}\\right] = \\tan^{-1}\\left[\\tan\\left(\\frac{\\theta}{2}\\right)\\right]\\)\n\\(u = \\frac{\\theta}{2} = \\frac{1}{2} \\tan^{-1} x\\)\nDifferentiate w.r.t. \\(x\\)\n\\(\\frac{du}{dx} = \\frac{1}{2} \\frac{d}{dx}(\\tan^{-1} x) = \\frac{1}{2(1+x^2)}\\) ... (II)\nAnd, \\(v = \\sin^{-1}\\left(\\frac{2x}{1+x^2}\\right) = \\sin^{-1}\\left(\\frac{2 \\tan \\theta}{1+\\tan^2 \\theta}\\right) = \\sin^{-1}(\\sin 2\\theta) = 2\\theta\\)\n\\(v = 2 \\tan^{-1} x\\)\nDifferentiate w.r.t. \\(x\\)\n\\(\\frac{dv}{dx} = 2 \\frac{d}{dx}(\\tan^{-1} x) = \\frac{2}{1+x^2}\\) ... (III)\nSubstituting (II) and (III) in (I) we get,\n\\(\\frac{du}{dv} = \\frac{\\frac{1}{2(1+x^2)}}{\\frac{2}{1+x^2}} = \\frac{1}{4}\\)"
